<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-21229] ptlrpc: writing threads_min does not start threads on a running service</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21229</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;Writing SERVICE.threads_min (e.g. ost.OSS.ost_io.threads_min) to a running service only sets srv_nthrs_cpt_init, which is read when the service is set up (ptlrpc_start_threads()).  A running service adds threads only when one of its threads wakes up and finds the others busy (ptlrpc_threads_need_create() in ptlrpc_main()), so a raised threads_min has no effect until the service is restarted.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This was seen in sanity-ec test_54n (&lt;a href="https://review.whamcloud.com/69709):" class="external-link" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener"&gt;https://review.whamcloud.com/69709):&lt;/a&gt; a zfs OSS that had just restarted ran 3 ost_io threads per CPU partition, and since a service with a high-priority handler keeps up to two of them free (ptlrpc_server_allow_normal()), two delayed reads of the same OSS were served one after the other.  Raising threads_min did not help.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Writing threads_min should start the missing threads in each CPU partition at once, up to threads_max.  The threads_min, threads_max and threads_started parameters also have no man4 pages.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
